WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.880
A 12 ounce latte sells for $5 while its beans, milk, and water cost roughly 20 cents.

00:00:06.420 --> 00:00:09.340
This is First Principles Daily, episode 92.

00:00:10.060 --> 00:00:14.820
The remaining difference does not come from any exotic material or rare processing step.

00:00:15.300 --> 00:00:21.200
It comes from the accumulated cost of storefront space, daily labor, equipment depreciation,

00:00:21.420 --> 00:00:26.200
and the decision to sell the drink already prepared rather than as ingredients a customer

00:00:26.200 --> 00:00:28.620
could combine at home for under 30 cents.

00:00:28.620 --> 00:00:34.460
That gap appears every time a customer pays the full retail price, instead of assembling

00:00:34.460 --> 00:00:37.440
the same drink from bulk commodities purchased separately.

00:00:38.660 --> 00:00:43.580
Specialty coffee shops price drinks to cover fixed and variable expenses that sit far above

00:00:43.580 --> 00:00:44.620
the commodity inputs.

00:00:45.420 --> 00:00:50.640
Rent for a visible street level location in a dense urban block often runs into the thousands

00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:54.860
of dollars per month, and that cost is spread across every cup sold.

00:00:55.580 --> 00:01:00.940
Buristas must be paid hourly wages plus benefits, and their time is spent not only pulling

00:01:00.940 --> 00:01:05.460
shots but also managing cues, cleaning equipment, and handling payments.

00:01:06.100 --> 00:01:11.460
Commercial espresso machines, grinders, and refrigeration units carry purchase prices and maintenance

00:01:11.460 --> 00:01:13.640
contracts that add further overhead.

00:01:14.280 --> 00:01:19.620
Milk must be delivered daily in refrigerated trucks, and any unsold product spoils.

00:01:20.340 --> 00:01:26.080
The shop also carries the cost of single-use cups, lids, sleeves, and napkins that customers

00:01:26.080 --> 00:01:27.900
expect as part of the transaction.

00:01:28.520 --> 00:01:33.220
These layers accumulate because the business model bundles the drink with immediate availability,

00:01:33.880 --> 00:01:36.720
a comfortable seating area, and a branded experience.

00:01:37.380 --> 00:01:42.500
Most customers accept the $5 price because they compare it to other prepared beverages rather

00:01:42.500 --> 00:01:44.940
than to the cost of replicating the drink themselves.

00:01:45.660 --> 00:01:50.940
The industry therefore treats the current margin structure as normal, rather than as a signal

00:01:50.940 --> 00:01:53.360
that the underlying steps could be rearranged.

00:01:53.780 --> 00:01:59.620
If every atom in the finished latte could be arranged instantly at no cost beyond the raw commodities,

00:02:00.280 --> 00:02:03.680
the price would sit near the value of the beans, milk, and water.

00:02:04.260 --> 00:02:07.880
Commodity coffee beans trade at roughly $2 to $4 per pound.

00:02:08.440 --> 00:02:13.800
A typical 12-ounce latte uses about 10 to 15 grams of grounds, which places the

00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:16.240
bean contribution in the range of a few cents.

00:02:16.820 --> 00:02:23.220
Whole milk at wholesale prices adds another 10 to 15 cents for the 6 to 8 ounces that go into the drink.

00:02:23.860 --> 00:02:29.200
Tap water and the negligible electricity or gas for heating complete the list, keeping the

00:02:29.200 --> 00:02:31.200
total material value around $0.20.

00:02:32.140 --> 00:02:38.820
Dividing the $5 retail price by that $0.20 floor produces an idiot index of roughly $0.25.

00:02:38.820 --> 00:02:44.540
That ratio shows the finished price is driven almost entirely by steps that occur after the

00:02:44.540 --> 00:02:50.400
materials exist. The largest single addition comes from the lease and fit out of the physical space,

00:02:50.820 --> 00:02:55.640
followed by the wages tied to onsite preparation and service. Daily deliveries,

00:02:56.080 --> 00:03:00.160
spoilage, and packaging each contribute smaller but recurring increments.

00:03:00.800 --> 00:03:06.300
The shop must also recover the capital cost of the espresso machine and the time spent training

00:03:06.300 --> 00:03:11.960
staff to operate it consistently. Because every one of these steps is performed at the point of

00:03:11.960 --> 00:03:16.940
sale rather than at a centralized facility, carrying costs compound through the day.

00:03:17.480 --> 00:03:23.160
The gap therefore lives in the sequence of operations, not in any shortage of the underlying commodities.

00:03:23.880 --> 00:03:28.300
A redesign would begin by separating the preparation step from the point of consumption.

00:03:29.040 --> 00:03:34.700
Concentrated coffee extract produced at industrial scale could be packaged in shelf stable form,

00:03:34.700 --> 00:03:38.620
removing the need for an espresso machine at every retail location.

00:03:39.220 --> 00:03:44.440
Shelf-stable milk concentrates or powdered formats would eliminate daily refrigerated deliveries

00:03:44.440 --> 00:03:50.760
and most spoilage. Customers could then reconstitute the drink at home or at a minimal dispensing

00:03:50.760 --> 00:03:56.740
station using only hot water and a measured packet. The first requirement for this path is

00:03:56.740 --> 00:04:02.820
consistent flavor quality after reconstitution. The second requirement is regulatory acceptance

00:04:02.820 --> 00:04:08.820
of the new formats for food service use. The third is scale sufficient to drive the cost of the

00:04:08.820 --> 00:04:14.680
concentrate and packaging below the current combined expense of beans, milk, labor, and rent.

00:04:15.440 --> 00:04:20.880
Early moves would likely target office buildings or transit hubs where foot traffic is predictable

00:04:20.880 --> 00:04:28.240
and seating is not required. Each of these changes attacks one layer that now inflates the 25 fold markup,

00:04:28.240 --> 00:04:34.160
but none removes the need for reliable cold chain alternatives or consumer willingness to perform

00:04:34.160 --> 00:04:39.720
the final mixing step. When the largest share of a product's price sits in the sequence of daily

00:04:39.720 --> 00:04:45.920
hand assembly and single-site storage rather than in its physical inputs, the opportunity lies in

00:04:45.920 --> 00:04:51.320
moving those steps earlier and farther from the customer. The same pattern appears whenever a

00:04:51.320 --> 00:04:56.520
commodity is sold only after it has been turned into a prepared good at the point of demand.

00:04:57.160 --> 00:05:02.260
The first signal that someone is testing this approach for coffee would be a pilot dispenser that

00:05:02.260 --> 00:05:08.860
delivers a consistent latte from a sealed concentrate cartridge in under 30 seconds. That's a wrap

00:05:08.860 --> 00:05:13.820
on today's first principles breakdown. The ideas travel further when you pass them on.

